1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,375
Who Pays for the News?

2
00:00:02,375 --> 00:00:06,525
Singapore Has Been Asking Since 1824

3
00:00:06,525 --> 00:00:09,665
Singapore's first newspaper was printed on

4
00:00:09,745 --> 00:00:13,345
rough Chinese-made paper,
folded into four small

5
00:00:13,425 --> 00:00:16,875
pages and sold for 18
Spanish dollars a year.

6
00:00:16,875 --> 00:00:20,984
It got through its first five
years on a government subsidy

7
00:00:21,064 --> 00:00:25,669
of 60 Spanish dollars a month, and
once that subsidy was withdrawn

8
00:00:25,749 --> 00:00:29,725
and competitors arrived, it
did not last another decade.

9
00:00:29,725 --> 00:00:35,354
Nearly two centuries later, the question
that decided the Singapore Chronicle's fate,

10
00:00:35,434 --> 00:00:38,175
who pays for local news in a small market,

11
00:00:38,255 --> 00:00:43,225
is being answered again, and public
money is once more part of the answer.

12
00:00:43,225 --> 00:00:46,490
The Singapore Chronicle's first issue

13
00:00:46,570 --> 00:00:49,825
appeared on the 1st of January 1824.

14
00:00:49,825 --> 00:00:54,020
The British Resident, John
Crawfurd, had applied in mid-1823

15
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:58,581
for permission to publish it on
behalf of Francis James Bernard,

16
00:00:58,661 --> 00:01:02,927
a former master attendant who
then ran the police department,

17
00:01:03,007 --> 00:01:06,561
and Crawfurd himself was
its principal contributor,

18
00:01:06,641 --> 00:01:10,275
so the paper functioned as
a semi-official gazette.

19
00:01:10,275 --> 00:01:14,307
It was printed at first by
the Mission Press and later on

20
00:01:14,387 --> 00:01:18,852
its own press at 26 Commercial
Square, on a single sheet folded

21
00:01:18,932 --> 00:01:22,964
into four quarto pages of
three columns each, and it came

22
00:01:23,044 --> 00:01:27,300
out fortnightly before moving
to a weekly schedule in 1830.

23
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:29,900
It was a paper for the port.

24
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:34,100
The Chronicle carried official
notifications, advertisements,

25
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:38,240
editorial notes on local events,
news from other countries,

26
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:42,600
letters from correspondents and
lists of imports and exports.

27
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:46,328
A year's subscription cost
18 Spanish dollars and a

28
00:01:46,408 --> 00:01:50,286
single copy cost 50 cents,
at a time when the Spanish

29
00:01:50,366 --> 00:01:53,950
dollar was still the coin
the whole town ran on.

30
00:01:53,950 --> 00:01:57,400
But subscriptions did not cover its costs.

31
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:02,647
The editor also received a fixed
government subsidy of 60 Spanish

32
00:02:02,727 --> 00:02:08,300
dollars a month, which was withdrawn
in 1829 in order to save money.

33
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:10,820
The larger change came in 1835,

34
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:14,675
when restrictions on
publishing were removed.

35
00:02:14,675 --> 00:02:19,571
That October, the Singapore Free
Press and Mercantile Advertiser began,

36
00:02:19,651 --> 00:02:23,575
in response to the sale of
the Chronicle to a new owner.

37
00:02:23,575 --> 00:02:27,615
Its founders included William
Napier and Edward Boustead,

38
00:02:27,695 --> 00:02:30,579
and its first editor was John Henry Moor,

39
00:02:30,659 --> 00:02:33,550
who had until then edited the Chronicle.

40
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:39,335
The older paper, now facing a rival
edited by its own former editor,

41
00:02:39,415 --> 00:02:43,900
ceased publication after
the 30th of September 1837.

42
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:48,026
The Straits Times and
Singapore Journal of Commerce

43
00:02:48,106 --> 00:02:52,726
launched on the 15th of July
1845 at 7 Commercial Square,

44
00:02:52,806 --> 00:02:55,775
a decade after the Free Press began.

45
00:02:55,775 --> 00:02:59,743
It was the work of an editor
from Bombay, Robert Carr Woods,

46
00:02:59,823 --> 00:03:02,510
and an Armenian merchant, Catchick Moses,

47
00:03:02,590 --> 00:03:06,356
who had bought printing
equipment originally ordered by a

48
00:03:06,436 --> 00:03:09,729
fellow Armenian merchant,
Marterus Thaddeus Apcar,

49
00:03:09,809 --> 00:03:13,925
before Apcar died without
realising his own publishing plans.

50
00:03:13,925 --> 00:03:18,305
The first issue ran to eight
folio pages, printed in new type

51
00:03:18,385 --> 00:03:22,625
on fine English paper, and
sold for half a Spanish dollar.

52
00:03:22,625 --> 00:03:25,854
It began as a Tuesday
weekly and went to twice

53
00:03:25,934 --> 00:03:29,675
weekly within four months,
though in a reduced size.

54
00:03:29,675 --> 00:03:32,303
Woods had struggled to convince Moses

55
00:03:32,383 --> 00:03:34,725
that the paper could make money.

56
00:03:34,725 --> 00:03:39,200
By September 1846, only
fourteen months after launch,

57
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:42,975
Moses tried to sell it
and found no buyers.

58
00:03:42,975 --> 00:03:47,650
The same question is now being
asked of a much larger industry,

59
00:03:47,730 --> 00:03:50,975
in a country of roughly
six million people.

60
00:03:50,975 --> 00:03:54,669
The publisher of the Straits
Times, Lianhe Zaobao,

61
00:03:54,749 --> 00:03:58,594
Berita Harian and Tamil
Murasu has been restructured

62
00:03:58,674 --> 00:04:01,915
into a not-for-profit
body, SPH Media Trust,

63
00:04:01,995 --> 00:04:06,750
which took over the media business
on the 1st of December 2021.

64
00:04:06,750 --> 00:04:10,747
On the 16th of February 2022
the government announced that

65
00:04:10,827 --> 00:04:15,597
it would provide up to 900 million
Singapore dollars over five years,

66
00:04:15,677 --> 00:04:18,619
about 180 million
Singapore dollars a year,

67
00:04:18,699 --> 00:04:22,907
tied to targets for the reach
and engagement of its products,

68
00:04:22,987 --> 00:04:27,275
including among vernacular-language
readers and young people.

69
00:04:27,275 --> 00:04:31,166
The Ministry of Digital
Development and Information

70
00:04:31,246 --> 00:04:35,761
reported that in the 2024
financial year the trust received

71
00:04:35,841 --> 00:04:39,499
260.6 million Singapore
dollars in base funding,

72
00:04:39,579 --> 00:04:44,404
plus up to 28.9 million Singapore
dollars in performance-linked

73
00:04:44,484 --> 00:04:48,300
incentives that depend on
meeting agreed targets.

74
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:52,715
The trust also disclosed
in 2023 that its reported

75
00:04:52,795 --> 00:04:56,940
daily circulation for
2022 had been inflated by

76
00:04:57,020 --> 00:05:01,794
between 85,000 and 95,000
copies, or 10 to 12 percent,

77
00:05:01,874 --> 00:05:05,650
through double counting and other methods.

78
00:05:05,650 --> 00:05:09,000
Television is on a similar footing.

79
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:13,429
Mediacorp, owned by the state
investment company Temasek,

80
00:05:13,509 --> 00:05:17,464
receives about 380 million
Singapore dollars a year

81
00:05:17,544 --> 00:05:22,527
in government allocations, the
minister for digital development,

82
00:05:22,607 --> 00:05:26,325
Josephine Teo, told
Parliament in October 2025.

83
00:05:26,325 --> 00:05:30,118
She set that against the
roughly 780 million Singapore

84
00:05:30,198 --> 00:05:35,569
dollars a year received by the national
broadcasters of Finland and Denmark,

85
00:05:35,649 --> 00:05:38,797
which she said have
similar population sizes,

86
00:05:38,877 --> 00:05:43,244
and said Mediacorp reached more
than 90 percent of Singapore's

87
00:05:43,324 --> 00:05:47,125
population across its own
and social media platforms.

88
00:05:47,125 --> 00:05:50,145
The government, she
added, now measures reach

89
00:05:50,225 --> 00:05:53,589
across all platforms rather
than television alone,

90
00:05:53,669 --> 00:05:56,700
reflecting how audience
habits have changed.

91
00:05:56,700 --> 00:06:01,325
The figures for online audiences
are of a different kind again.

92
00:06:01,325 --> 00:06:05,947
CNA, Mediacorp's news brand,
reported nearly 12 million

93
00:06:06,027 --> 00:06:09,879
unique visitors a month
to its website between

94
00:06:09,959 --> 00:06:14,575
January and July 2025,
according to its own analytics.

95
00:06:14,575 --> 00:06:18,359
The Straits Times put its website online

96
00:06:18,439 --> 00:06:22,400
in 1994 and introduced a paywall in 2005.

97
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:26,346
Visitors, subscribers and printed
copies are not the same thing,

98
00:06:26,426 --> 00:06:29,680
so these numbers cannot be
ranked against each other,

99
00:06:29,760 --> 00:06:32,952
but they show that the
traditional and the broadcast

100
00:06:33,032 --> 00:06:36,726
newsrooms are now competing for
attention in the same place,

101
00:06:36,806 --> 00:06:39,700
and that both are supported
with public money.

102
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:42,681
The stated rationale for that support is

103
00:06:42,761 --> 00:06:45,975
about trust and reach rather than control.

104
00:06:45,975 --> 00:06:49,558
Minister Teo has said that
"no one gains if these

105
00:06:49,638 --> 00:06:53,825
products lack credibility and
are ignored by audiences."

106
00:06:53,825 --> 00:06:57,838
Research for the Reuters Institute
for the Study of Journalism,

107
00:06:57,918 --> 00:07:01,931
drawing on interviews with
journalists, recorded concerns about

108
00:07:02,011 --> 00:07:06,869
deference to authority and government
communications influence in newsrooms,

109
00:07:06,949 --> 00:07:10,572
while an SPH Media editor
countered that the relationship

110
00:07:10,652 --> 00:07:14,550
with the government had not
changed after the restructuring.

111
00:07:14,550 --> 00:07:18,268
Whether public funding
preserves a local view of events

112
00:07:18,348 --> 00:07:21,929
or shapes it is a question
people answer differently,

113
00:07:22,009 --> 00:07:25,600
and the Chronicle's story
suggests it is an old one.

114
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:28,586
Where it fits in the
bigger story: In 1824,

115
00:07:28,666 --> 00:07:33,933
a colonial government paid a printer
60 dollars a month so that its notices

116
00:07:34,013 --> 00:07:37,426
and the port's shipping
lists would be published,

117
00:07:37,506 --> 00:07:42,425
and after the subsidy ended and
competition arrived the paper folded.

118
00:07:42,425 --> 00:07:45,270
In 1846 the paper nobody would

119
00:07:45,350 --> 00:07:48,275
buy was the one that survived.

120
00:07:48,275 --> 00:07:52,395
Small markets have always struggled
to pay for their own news,

121
00:07:52,475 --> 00:07:55,307
and Singapore's two
centuries of newspapers

122
00:07:55,387 --> 00:07:58,355
show the same tension
between subsidy and the

123
00:07:58,435 --> 00:08:02,025
open market from the very
first issue to the present.
